The method copies the entire `requestHeaders` map into a new `HashMap` only to retain two specific keys. If a request carries many headers, this allocates and copies all of them unnecessarily. Since the goal is to log at most two known keys, a targeted lookup avoids the full copy:
```java
Map<String, String> selectedHeaders = new HashMap<>(2);
String opaqueId = requestHeaders.get(Task.X_OPAQUE_ID_HTTP_HEADER);
if (opaqueId != null) selectedHeaders.put(Task.X_OPAQUE_ID_HTTP_HEADER, opaqueId);
String productOrigin = requestHeaders.get(Task.X_ELASTIC_PRODUCT_ORIGIN_HTTP_HEADER);
if (productOrigin != null) selectedHeaders.put(Task.X_ELASTIC_PRODUCT_ORIGIN_HTTP_HEADER, productOrigin);
```
This is a minor point since the code sits on a warning path, but it avoids O(n) allocation for a constant-size result.

